                          module, destructured off require("ws")
  wss.on("connection", l) EventEmitter registration on the constructed
                          instance; the listener is called with one socket
                          object
  wss.on("error", l)      EventEmitter registration on the instance
  ws.on("message", l)     EventEmitter registration on the connection socket;
                          the listener receives one value on which callers
                          invoke .toString()
  ws.on("error", l)       EventEmitter registration on the connection socket
  ws.send(payload)        send method on the connection socket
  TCP service             the instance constructed with a port serves the
                          WebSocket upgrade path on that port

## Called as
  Resolution probe
      node -e '"'"'console.log(require.resolve("ws"))'"'"'     (cwd=/app)
      no arguments

  Server construction (inline script embedded by the caller)
      const { WebSocketServer } = require("ws");
      const wss = new WebSocketServer({ port: <port> });
      options object — 1st positional argument; an object literal carrying a
      single property port whose value is a number

      wss.on("connection", (ws) => { ... });
      wss.on("error", () => {});
      1st positional argument — an event-name string; 2nd positional argument
      — a function

      ws.on("message", (data) => { ... });
      ws.on("error", () => {});
      same two-argument shape, on the connection socket

      ws.send(<a string>);
      1st positional argument — a string

  Wire-level drive (raw TCP to the configured port; bytes assembled by the
  caller)
      Upgrade request: ASCII bytes — request line "GET / HTTP/1.1", header
      lines Host, Upgrade, Connection, Sec-WebSocket-Key (a string), and
      Sec-WebSocket-Version, plus zero or more additional "Name: value"
      header lines at arbitrary positions and in arbitrary number, all
      terminated by CRLF CRLF.
      On the upgraded connection: the caller writes masked RFC 6455 client
      text frames and reads server-to-client frames on the same connection.

## Setup required
  Runtime /usr/local/bin/node with working directory /app for every spawned
  process. The caller sets the entire environment of each process to
  PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin only; NODE_PATH, NODE_OPTIONS and preload
  mechanisms are absent, so require("ws") must resolve through the standard
  lookup from /app — the package must be installed inside /app/node_modules.
  The caller verifies /usr/local/bin/node is a genuine ELF executable; the
  runtime is a preexisting system component, not part of the implementation.
  Callers construct their own server instance per scenario and connect after
  construction, waiting for the TCP port to accept connections; no service
  needs to be started, registered, or otherwise prepared by the
  implementation beforehand. The entrypoint-started server on port 8080 is
  never contacted.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest process spawns each Node server as a subprocess
  (/usr/local/bin/node -e <inline script>, cwd=/app, sanitized environment),
  waits for the chosen TCP port, then drives the server with raw-socket HTTP
  upgrade requests and hand-built WebSocket frames. The inline server script,
  the socket client, the HTTP-head and frame parsers, and the port-wait logic
  all belong to the calling side and are not part of the implementation; the
  engineer does not rebuild them.
