## Reached at
  The implementation is the vendored JavaScript library files on disk under /app,
  staged into the image by the build; the project treats them as its own runtime
  library files, and the callers pin the page to exactly those bytes:
      /app/vuetify.js        Vuetify 2.6.9 UMD dist build
      /app/vue.js            Vue.js 2.6.14 UMD dist build
      /app/vuetify.min.css   stylesheet
  Immediately before the suites run, the calling side copies these three files
  byte for byte into the directory it serves and records their size and sha256.
  Two ephemeral local HTTP servers, rooted at that served directory, are started
  for the duration of the run:
      http://localhost:8765
      http://127.0.0.1:8766
  A headless Chromium browser, driven through Selenium, navigates to the
  calendar page served from each server. Both suites travel the same surface:
  that page loads the staged copies of the library and exercises a v-calendar
  component through its :events array plus a custom :event-name function
  callback. The page loads the implementation as classic scripts, exactly:
      <link href="/vuetify.min.css" rel="stylesheet">
      <script src="/vue.js"></script>
      <script src="/vuetify.js"></script>
  and mounts it as:
      new Vue({ el: '"'"'#app'"'"', vuetify: new Vuetify(), ... })
  There is no module loader, bundler, or build step between /app and what the
  page executes. Each suite confirms the surface is genuinely traveled: the
  library files are actually requested from the served directory, and the
  calendar renders its events through the :event-name callback route. One
  caller additionally makes plain HTTP GET requests for /vue.js, /vuetify.js
  and /vuetify.min.css and compares the served bytes against the recorded
  size/sha256 of the /app copies, so the page cannot be running a substitute
  library that differs from the files on disk at /app.

## Must provide
  /app/vue.js            non-empty file; loaded as a classic script; defines the
                         global Vue
  /app/vuetify.js        non-empty file; UMD dist build loaded as a classic
                         script after vue.js; resolves its Vue dependency from
                         the global and defines the global Vuetify
  /app/vuetify.min.css   non-empty stylesheet file
  window.Vue             defined in the page once the scripts have loaded
  window.Vuetify         defined in the page; constructible as new Vuetify()
                         and accepted as the vuetify option of a Vue instance
  v-calendar             component registered through the Vuetify plugin; the
                         page also uses v-app and the v-container / v-row /
                         v-col layout components around it
  v-calendar props the caller binds:
      events      Array of event objects
      event-name  String or Function (bound as :event-name)
      type        String
      start       String
      color       String
  Rendered identities the callers resolve with CSS selectors:
      .v-calendar              root element of the rendered calendar
      .v-calendar-daily__day   day columns of the week view
      .v-event, .v-event-timed one element per rendered event; event elements
                               carry a background color derived from the
                               event'"'"'s color field
  event-name callback contract: the library invokes the function as
  (event, timedEvent); the first argument exposes .input, the raw event object
  taken from the events array. The string the callback returns is the display
  name of the rendered event, read by callers through the event elements'"'"'
  text content.

