## Reached at
  Callers exec GPAC'"'"'s MP4Box command-line tool by absolute path, with no
  shell, no interpreter, and a per-run working directory:

      subprocess.run(["/app/bin/gcc/MP4Box"] + args, cwd=workdir,
                     stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
                     timeout=timeout,
                     env={"PATH": "/usr/local/sbin:...:/bin",
                          "HOME": workdir})

  /app/bin/gcc/MP4Box is a build artifact of the GPAC source tree at /app.
  The image build clones the upstream GPAC repository into /app at a pinned
  commit, removes .git, and builds in place with `./configure &&
  make -j$(nproc)`; the binary lands at bin/gcc/MP4Box. /usr/local/bin/MP4Box
  is a symlink to that same file, but callers use the /app path directly.
  The binary resolves its own libgpac via RUNPATH=$ORIGIN, i.e. the shared
  library that `make` places next to it; nothing outside /app/bin/gcc is
  needed to run it.

  Working directory: every invocation runs inside a fresh, empty temporary
  directory created per run by the caller. Input files are written into that
  directory first and relative output names resolve against it.

## Must provide
  /app/bin/gcc/MP4Box        an executable regular file at exactly this path;
                             it is what `make` in /app regenerates, and it is
                             the path the callers check and exec
  -version                   identifies the tool and the project it belongs to
  -add <file> -new <file>    compile a scene description file into an ISOM
                             file written at the -new path; the scene input
                             is a text file addressed by path (.bt BT scene
                             or .xmt XML scene)
  -info <file>               report information about an ISOM file addressed
                             by path
  -dash-live <n> <inputs>    run a live DASH session over the given inputs;
                             the inputs are file paths and include both media
                             files (.mp4) and scene files (.bt, .xmt)
  -out <name>                with -dash-live: the manifest name, resolved
                             against the process working directory
  DASH session artifacts     media segments matching `*_dash*.m4s` and an
                             initialization segment matching `*init.mp4`,
                             written into the process working directory

## Called as
  MP4Box -version
      no inputs.

  MP4Box -add scene_path -new out_path
      scene_path   absolute path (string) to a text scene file written by the
                   caller, extension .bt or .xmt
      out_path     absolute path (string) naming the ISOM file to write

  MP4Box -info file_path
      file_path    absolute path (string) to an ISOM file

  MP4Box -dash-live 1000 -out live.mpd media_path
      1000         value of the -dash-live option; a string of decimal digits
      live.mpd     value of -out; a bare filename resolved against the
                   process working directory
      media_path   trailing positional; absolute path (string) to a media file
      The caller applies a wall-clock timeout here and treats a session still
      running at expiry as the live mode operating: the process is killed on
      expiry and the caller then inspects the working directory for the
      manifest and segment artifacts.

  MP4Box -dash-live 1000 probe_path
      probe_path   trailing positional; absolute path (string) to a .bt or
                   .xmt file inside the per-run temporary directory, under a
                   fresh randomized file name chosen per run (and, in one
                   case, a copy of a fixture shipped with the caller)
      The caller accepts a run as having reached the implementation only if
      the base name of this input file appears in the program'"'"'s combined
      stdout+stderr; the tool names its input session entries after the input
      file, which is what makes the contact observable.

## Setup required
  - /app must remain the GPAC build tree: after source changes, `make` in
    /app must regenerate bin/gcc/MP4Box at that exact path. Callers exec that
    path directly; a binary anywhere else, or a stale artifact that `make`
    does not refresh, never answers the calls.
  - The binary is launched with an environment containing only PATH and HOME
    (HOME set to the per-run temporary working directory): no
    LD_LIBRARY_PATH, no LD_PRELOAD, no pre-existing GPAC configuration.
    Library resolution must come from the binary'"'"'s own RUNPATH.
  - Callers assume no system-wide dynamic-linker preload file
    (/etc/ld.so.preload) is installed; they check for it and do not trust
    observations made with one present.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest driver that spawns the binary through
  `subprocess.run` with an argv list, the absolute binary path, a controlled
  working directory, a reduced environment, and per-call wall-clock timeouts.
  It creates the per-run temporary working directories, writes text scene
  files into them, copies a shipped scene fixture under a randomized name,
  and reads the manifest and segment files back out of those same
  directories. A media fixture (.mp4) and a scene fixture (.bt) ship beside
  the driver and are referenced by path relative to the driver'"'"'s own
  directory. None of this — the driver, the fixtures, the temporary
  directories, the timeout handling — is part of the implementation; do not
  rebuild any of it.
```

