## Reached at
  A CommonJS package loaded by absolute path under the Node interpreter
  /usr/local/bin/node (preinstalled in the base image; CommonJS runtime):
      const Sequelize = require('"'"'/app/index.js'"'"');
  The callers resolve the entry with `require.resolve('"'"'/app/index.js'"'"')`, take
  `fs.realpathSync` of it, and require the real path; they check that this real
  path lies inside the /app tree before trusting anything the module does. The
  working directory of the calling process does not matter: resolution is by
  absolute path and the caller'"'"'s scripts run from /tmp.
  /app/index.js is the package main of the Sequelize ORM tree staged at /app by
  the image build (package name `sequelize`, `"main": "index.js"`); it does
  `module.exports = require('"'"'./lib/sequelize'"'"')`, so the module export is the
  Sequelize class itself. The tree'"'"'s own package.json and lib/ modules are
  project-owned; the Node interpreter and everything under /app/node_modules
  (lodash, bluebird, the sqlite3/mysql2/mariadb drivers, ...) are preinstalled
  prerequisites, not code the implementation owns.

## Must provide
  The exported Sequelize class at /app/index.js, with:
    Sequelize.json                     static method on the class (also reachable
                                       from instances); builds the JSON condition/
                                       path object that every call below passes in
    Sequelize.DataTypes.STRING         static type reference
    Sequelize.DataTypes.JSON           static type reference
    new Sequelize(...)                 constructor; the instance exposes
                                       instance.dialect.QueryGenerator
  The per-dialect QueryGenerator objects, reachable as
  instance.dialect.QueryGenerator for options.dialect '"'"'mysql'"'"', '"'"'mariadb'"'"' and
  '"'"'sqlite'"'"'. The JSON handling the callers reach lives in project-owned code:
  lib/dialects/mysql/query-generator.js, lib/dialects/mariadb/query-generator.js
  and lib/dialects/sqlite/query-generator.js, on top of the shared base in
  lib/dialects/abstract/query-generator.js (which supplies whereItemQuery and
  selectQuery to all three):
    handleSequelizeMethod(smth)       synchronous method on the generator
    whereItemQuery(key, value)         synchronous method on the generator
    selectQuery(tableName, options)    synchronous method on the generator
  The Model route on a live instance (used end-to-end with dialect '"'"'sqlite'"'"'):
    sequelize.define(name, attrs, opts) returns a Model class
    sequelize.sync()                   async (promise), no arguments
    Model.create(values)               async (promise)
    Model.findAll([options])           async (promise)
    sequelize.close()                  async (promise)

## Called as
  Sequelize.json(conditionsOrPath[, value])
      conditionsOrPath — 1st positional argument; either a string (a column
                         name followed by dot-separated path segments) or a
                         plain nested object (column name -> path segment ->
                         value, arbitrarily nested)
      value            — optional 2nd positional argument; a string or a number
  new Sequelize(database, username, password, options)
      database, username, password — first three positional arguments; strings
                                     (username and password may also be null)
      options — plain object; the callers pass `dialect` (the string '"'"'mysql'"'"',
                '"'"'mariadb'"'"' or '"'"'sqlite'"'"'), `logging` (a function or false), and
                for the sqlite route `storage` (a string; the callers pass
                '"'"':memory:'"'"')
      Generator surface, on the value of
      `new Sequelize('"'"'test'"'"', '"'"'user'"'"', '"'"'pass'"'"', opts).dialect.QueryGenerator`
      (aliased `qg` below):
  qg.handleSequelizeMethod(smth)
      smth — 1st positional argument; an object returned by Sequelize.json(...);
             the callers pass no further arguments
  qg.whereItemQuery(key, value)
      key   — 1st positional argument; the callers pass `undefined`
      value — 2nd positional argument; an object returned by Sequelize.json(...)
  qg.selectQuery(tableName, options)
      tableName — 1st positional argument; a string (a table name)
      options   — 2nd positional argument; a plain object whose `where` value is
                  an object returned by Sequelize.json(...)
      Model route, on a sqlite instance created with `storage: '"'"':memory:'"'"'`:
  sequelize.define(name, attributes, options)
      name       — 1st positional argument; a string
      attributes — 2nd positional argument; a plain object mapping attribute
                   names to Sequelize.DataTypes members (callers use
                   DataTypes.STRING and DataTypes.JSON)
      options    — 3rd positional argument; a plain object with a `tableName`
                   string
  Model.create(values)       values — 1st positional argument; a plain object
                             mapping the defined attribute names to values
                             (strings and plain nested objects)
  Model.findAll()            no arguments
  Model.findAll(options)     options — 1st positional argument; a plain object
                             whose `where` value is an object returned by
                             Sequelize.json(...)
  sequelize.sync() / sequelize.close()   no arguments
  The calling side consumes the generator calls'"'"' produced value as SQL text: it
  requires that value to be a string, reads it as a SQL fragment, and on the
  sqlite end-to-end route the produced condition fragment is joined into a
  SELECT statement that is executed against a real SQLite database through the
  driver resolved at /app/node_modules/sqlite3.

## Setup required
  - The package tree must live at /app and resolve there: the callers take the
    real path of the resolved entry and proceed only when it is under /app/,
    so a copy placed elsewhere in the module lookup path cannot satisfy them.
  - The callers run the interpreter with a scrubbed environment (only
